## Proof

**Claim:** Yes, $a^q = a$ for all $a \in D$.

**Proof.** Let $D$ be a finite division ring with $|D| = q$. Consider the set
$$D^\times = D \setminus \{0\}.$$
Since $D$ is a division ring, every nonzero element has a multiplicative inverse, so $D^\times$ is a group under multiplication (the identity is $1 \in D$). This group is finite, with
$$|D^\times| = q - 1.$$

Note that we use **only** the group axioms for $D^\times$—multiplicative associativity, identity, and inverses—all of which hold in any division ring. Commutativity is **not** required.

By **Lagrange's theorem** (which applies to every finite group, abelian or not), the order of every element of $D^\times$ divides $|D^\times| = q - 1$. Hence for every $a \in D^\times$,
$$a^{q-1} = 1.$$
Multiplying both sides by $a$,
$$a^q = a \qquad \text{for all } a \in D^\times = D \setminus \{0\}.$$

It remains to check $a = 0$. Since $q \geq 2$ (a division ring has at least $0$ and $1$), $q \geq 1$, so
$$0^q = 0.$$

Combining both cases,
$$a^q = a \qquad \text{for all } a \in D. \qquad \blacksquare$$

**Remark.** This argument does not use Wedderburn's little theorem (that every finite division ring is commutative). The identity $a^q = a$ holds in *any* finite division ring purely as a consequence of Lagrange's theorem applied to the (possibly non-abelian) multiplicative group $D^\times$.

